Move $\sqrt{x}$ to the right-hand side and square both sides to get rid of the square root on the right hand side. $$
   \begin{aligned}
      \sqrt{x + 7} &= \sqrt{x} + \sqrt{3}, \\
      x + 7 &= x + 2\sqrt{3x} + 3. \\
   \end{aligned}
   $$ Simplifying $x$ cancels out, leaving us with $$2\sqrt{3x} = 4.$$
      Square both sides again to get rid of the radical. $$
   \begin{aligned}
      4 \cdot 3x &= 16, \\
      12x &= 16, \\
      x &= \frac{4}{3}.
   \end{aligned}
   $$ $ $ Next, we need to check if $\frac{4}{3}$ satisfies the original equation. $$
   \begin{aligned}
      \sqrt{\frac{4}{3} + 7} - \sqrt{\frac{4}{3}} &= \sqrt{3}, \\
      \sqrt{\frac{25}{3}} - \sqrt{\frac{4}{3}} &= \sqrt{3}, \\
      \sqrt{\frac{25}{3}} - \sqrt{\frac{4}{3}} &= \sqrt{3}. \\
   \end{aligned}
   $$ Since the left-hand side is equal to the right-hand side, $\boxed{\frac{4}{3}}$ is a valid solution. </think> <answer> $\boxed{\frac{4}{3}}$ </answer>

